# Academic Passage Reading and Skimming

> Verify the live Reading format, computer-delivered vs paper-based delivery, and the 60-minute timing policy at your chosen test centre in Qatar on https://ielts.org/ and https://www.britishcouncil.qa/ before planning revision.

---

### 🟢 Lite — Quick Review (1h–1d)

#### Core facts in one pass

- **Format:** 3 academic passages. 13-14 questions per passage. 40 questions total. 60 minutes (no extra transfer time — Reading is paper-based or computer-based with no separate transfer window).
- **Passage source:** books, journals, magazines, newspapers. NOT fiction, NOT poetry, NOT song lyrics. Academic register at all times.
- **Total word count:** 2,150-2,750 words across the three passages. Passage 1 is the easiest (typically a working-academic passage), Passage 2 is medium (often a process or argument), Passage 3 is the hardest (often an academic argument or research summary).
- **Question types:** 11 official question types — multiple choice, identifying information, identifying writer's views, matching information, matching headings, matching features, matching sentence endings, sentence completion, summary completion, note/table/diagram completion, short-answer. Each is covered in a separate topic row.
- **Order of answers:** questions follow the passage in order. Most question types go IN ORDER through the passage; matching headings goes paragraph-by-paragraph.
- **Marking:** +1 correct, 0 incorrect (no negative marking). Spelling errors are accepted if the meaning is unchanged.
- **Predictable content:** academic vocabulary at register; complex sentence structure with subordinate clauses; passive voice; nominalisation; abstract concepts with concrete examples.

#### The three core reading strategies

| Strategy | Speed | Purpose | Used for |
|---|---|---|---|
| Skimming | fast (~3-4 sec/paragraph) | identify the gist/main point | getting the overall structure of the passage |
| Scanning | fast (~1-2 sec/line) | find specific information (numbers, names, dates) | locating details for completion questions |
| Reading for detail | slow (variable) | understand every word | answering True/False/Not Given, multiple choice, matching features |

---

#### Examiner traps

- Reading the passage FIRST without reading the questions. The binding constraint in Reading is time; skimming without purpose wastes 8-12 minutes.
- Reading the passage SLOWLY. Reading for detail on the first read wastes 15-20 minutes and leaves no time for the questions.
- Skipping the questions' order. Most question types follow the passage in order; the candidate who reads out of order wastes time hunting for answers.
- Falling for synonyms. The passage paraphrases the question; the candidate must recognise the synonym to find the answer.
- Confusing True/False/Not Given with Yes/No/Not Given. True/False is about FACT (does the statement agree with the passage); Yes/No is about VIEW (does the writer hold this view). They are NOT interchangeable.

