### Previous Year Patterns — IELTS Reading

The IELTS Reading test format has been structurally stable across every IELTS cycle since the Academic / General Training split in 1989. The three-passage format (40 questions, 60 minutes, 2,150-2,750 words) is published verbatim on ielts.org and is not expected to change.

Common year-on-year patterns:
- The 40-question count has been stable since the early 2000s.
- Passage 1 is typically a working-academic passage (the easiest of the three); Passage 2 is medium (often a process or argument); Passage 3 is the hardest (often an academic argument or research summary).
- The 11 question families recur across cycles; weighting shifts slightly but no family is ever absent.
- A previous-year passage is NOT reused verbatim in the same year; recycled passages appear with at most small contextual changes.

A previous-year record is not a deterministic indicator of a future test item. The British Council and IDP refresh the question bank annually, calibrated against the same difficulty curve.

